# Freshservice create

## Gate

Ordinary coding stays **ticketless**. Do not create a ticket, invent an id, or
open the service desk to “cover” work unless this turn explicitly authorizes
it.

Create **only** when the user text clearly says to file one, for example:

- “make a Freshservice ticket”, “file an incident”, “open a service request”,
  “create a problem/change”, “file it in the service desk”

These do **not** authorize create — just implement on the current branch:

- “add X”, “fix Y”, “I think Z is broken”, naming a feature without filing

Naming an **existing** ticket id means work that ticket — it does not mean
create another one.

Ambiguity → **do not create**. Never ask whether to file unless the user
already raised filing.

## Create-only is the default

“File a Freshservice ticket / incident” authorizes **create only**. Put the
rest of the message into the description (see `freshservice-ticket`) and
**stop**. Do not edit code, open a worktree, or change status beyond the
default created state.

Create-only stays create-only when the primary verb this turn is file or
ticket. Do not treat that ask as authorization to implement.

## Create-and-implement (explicit second verb only)

Same-turn implementation only when the user adds an explicit second verb, for
example: “file it and fix it”, “create the incident and remediate”, “file it
then implement”. Inference (“they described the outage, so fix prod”) is
forbidden.

## Defaults (never invent)

When create is authorized:

1. **Workspace / domain** — host-configured default when present; else a
   workspace the operator or chat already named. Never invent a domain.
2. **Group / requester / category / priority** — same rule: configured or
   named only; omit or leave host defaults when unknown.
3. **Type** — see Types below; do not invent custom types the workspace does
   not use.

Prefer a connected Freshservice / ITSM MCP when one is ready. If none is
connected, use the operator’s configured API or UI path once — **do not
invent credentials, tool names, or side CLIs**.

## Types

| Type | Default create? | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Incident | Yes when user says incident / outage / broken | Impact + urgency honest |
| Service request | Yes when user says request / access / provision | Requested outcome clear |
| Problem | Only if user asks for a problem | Root-cause tracking; link incidents when known |
| Change | Only if user asks for a change | Window / risk / rollback when known |

If the user says only “ticket” without a type, prefer **Incident** for
breakage and **Service request** for asks — state which you chose.

## Create procedure

1. Resolve workspace / group / requester / category / priority from host
   config or explicit context — never invent.
2. Apply `freshservice-ticket` for the description body.
3. Create via connected MCP when available; otherwise the operator’s
   configured API/UI path. Do not invent tool names.
4. Leave in the **default created / Open** state unless the user asked to
   assign, prioritize, or resolve. Status moves: `freshservice-status`.
5. Return the ticket id and browse link when the site URL is known.

## Done

Create is done when the reply contains the ticket id (or a clear create
failure), the body matched `freshservice-ticket`, and no product code was
edited on a create-only ask.
